{===========================================================================
  Iron block quenched in a water tank (exergy destruction)
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0056
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A 5 kg iron block initially at 350 degC is quenched in an insulated rigid
  tank containing 100 kg of water at 30 degC; the surroundings (dead state)
  are at 20 degC and 100 kPa. The water and the block are incompressible
  substances with constant specific heats; kinetic and potential energy
  changes and every form of work are neglected; the tank is adiabatic and
  any vapour formed is assumed to condense back in it.

  A first law on the combined system (closed, rigid, adiabatic) gives the
  final equilibrium temperature T_f. The exergy of the combined system is
  then evaluated at the initial and final states, and their difference is
  the exergy destruction (wasted work potential) of the process.

  Inputs : m_b, m_w, T_b_i, T_w_i, T_0, p_0, c_b, c_w
  Outputs: T_f, X_tot_i, X_tot_f, X_destroyed
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ULiège, course Thermodynamique appliquée (MECA0002), repetition
           session 10, exercise 1 (R10_E01_2022.EES) - see README.md
===========================================================================}

"!Problem statement (paraphrased from the original)"
"A 5 kg block initially at 350 °C is quenched in an insulated tank containing
100 kg of water at 30 °C. The water that vaporises during the process is
assumed to condense back in the tank. The surroundings are at 20 °C and
100 kPa. Determine (a) the final equilibrium temperature and (b) the wasted
work potential of the process."

"!Assumptions, as in the original"
"1) the water and the iron block are incompressible substances; 2) constant
specific heats (evaluated near ambient temperature) are used for both;
3) the changes of kinetic and potential energy are nil; 4) no electrical,
shaft or other form of work is involved; 5) the system is thermally
insulated (adiabatic)."

"!Given data"
m_b = 5 [kg]              "mass of the iron block"
m_w = 100 [kg]            "mass of the water"
T_b_i = 350 [C]           "initial temperature of the block (623.15 K in the original)"
T_w_i = 30 [C]            "initial temperature of the water (303.15 K in the original)"
T_0 = 20 [C]              "temperature of the environment, taken as reference (293.15 K in the original)"
p_0 = 100E3 [Pa]          "ambient pressure (100 kPa in the original); not used in the equations (rigid tank), as in the original"
c_b = 450 [J/kg-K]        "specific heat of the iron block (0.450 kJ/kg-K in the original)"
c_w = 4180 [J/kg-K]       "specific heat of the water (4.180 kJ/kg-K in the original)"

"!First law on the combined system (closed, rigid, adiabatic): DELTAU_system = 0"
m_b*c_b*(T_f-T_b_i) = -m_w*c_w*(T_f-T_w_i)   "the block gives its heat to the water, as in the original"

"!Absolute temperatures (the exergy terms below need them)"
T_b_i_K = T_b_i + 273.15  "initial temperature of the block [K]"
T_w_i_K = T_w_i + 273.15  "initial temperature of the water [K]"
T_0_K = T_0 + 273.15      "dead-state temperature [K]"
T_f_K = T_f + 273.15      "final equilibrium temperature [K]"

"!Exergy of the combined system, initial and final states"
"As in the original: EES could compute the internal energy and entropy
directly (also for the block, substance 'Iron'); the analytical method below
assumes constant specific heats. For an incompressible substance, with the
rigid tank (dV = 0), the Gibbs relation gives (s-s_0) = c*ln(T/T_0) and the
closed-system exergy is X = m*c*((T - T_0) - T_0*ln(T/T_0))."
X_b_i = m_b*c_b*(T_b_i_K - T_0_K - T_0_K*ln(T_b_i_K/T_0_K))   "exergy of the block, initial state [J]"
X_w_i = m_w*c_w*(T_w_i_K - T_0_K - T_0_K*ln(T_w_i_K/T_0_K))   "exergy of the water, initial state [J]"
X_tot_i = X_b_i + X_w_i                                        "exergy of the combined system, initial state [J]"
X_b_f = m_b*c_b*(T_f_K - T_0_K - T_0_K*ln(T_f_K/T_0_K))       "exergy of the block, final state [J]"
X_w_f = m_w*c_w*(T_f_K - T_0_K - T_0_K*ln(T_f_K/T_0_K))       "exergy of the water, final state [J]"
X_tot_f = X_b_f + X_w_f                                        "exergy of the combined system, final state [J]"

"!Exergy balance on the isolated system: X_in - X_out - X_destroyed = DELTAX_system"
"No exergy enters or leaves the isolated system, so:"
X_destroyed = X_tot_i - X_tot_f   "exergy destruction = wasted work potential [J]"
X_destroyed_bis = m_b*c_b*(T_b_i_K-T_f_K - T_0_K*ln(T_b_i_K/T_f_K))+m_w*c_w*(T_w_i_K-T_f_K - T_0_K*ln(T_w_i_K/T_f_K))   "the same exergy destruction written directly, as in the original [J]"
